      "text": "We are given the temperature function \\( T(t) = -t^2 + 12t + 50 \\) and we need to find the largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees. This means we need to solve the equation:\n\n\\[ -t^2 + 12t + 50 = 77 \\]\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation to set it to zero:\n\n\\[ -t^2 + 12t + 50 - 77 = 0 \\]\n\\[ -t^2 + 12t - 27 = 0 \\]\n\nNext, we multiply the entire equation by -1 to simplify:\n\n\\[ t^2 - 12t + 27 = 0 \\]\n\nNow, we solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -12 \\), and \\( c = 27 \\).\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant:\n\n\\[ b^2 - 4ac = (-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 27 = 144 - 108 = 36 \\]\n\nNow, we apply the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[ t = \\frac{-(-12) \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2 \\cdot 1} \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 \\pm 6}{2} \\]\n\nThis gives us two possible solutions:\n\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 + 6}{2} = \\frac{18}{2} = 9 \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 - 6}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3 \\]\n\nThe largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees is \\( t = 9 \\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
